## 1 Introduction
Owing to the low cost and globally inexhaustible resources of sodium element, the emerging sodium-ion batteries (SIBs) have been recently regarded as a promising alternative or good complement to conventional lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) for renewable grid-scale energy storage applications [1–3]. Nevertheless, the current challenges, such as low specific capacity, poor cycling and rate performance mainly associated with the larger ionic radius $\left(0.102{\mathrm{nm}}\right)$ and heavier atomic mass of sodium than that of lithium, severely impede their practical employment [4–6]. Many electrode materials utilized for lithium-ion chemistry are proved to be less electroactive or even completely inactive in SIBs [7,8]. Thus, it is significant and imperative to exploit appropriate anode materials to well match the relatively mature cathode systems with more stable and effective accommodation of sodium ions.
Among the limited number of anode materials, including hard carbon [9,10], metal alloys [11–14], transition-metal oxides/sulfides [15–17], tin disulfide $\mathrm{(SnS}_{2})$ with unique lamellar structure and considerably large interlayer spacing of $0.59\mathrm{nm}$ has stimulated tremendous research attention in the past decade on account of its high theoretical capacity $(1,136\mathrm{\mAh\g}^{-1})$ induced by combined conversion and alloying reactions, natural abundance and environmental benignity [18]. However, $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ usually suffers from the main bottlenecks of intrinsically low electrical conductivity, sluggish ion diffusion kinetics and drastic volume changes $(\sim324\%)$ during cycling, which inevitably result in the large discrepancy in capacities between the practical and theoretical values, fast capacity fading and inferior structural durability [19,20].
To circumvent the above issues, several effective strategies, including nanostructure engineering [21,22], heteroatom incorporation [23,24] and integrating with carbonaceous materials [25,26], have been adopted to enhance the sodium storage performance of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in SIBs. For instance, Xiong et al. [27] reported that ultrafine $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanocrystals on aminofunctionalized graphene with strong C–N–Sn bonding were fabricated by an amine-thermal reaction, exhibiting a reversible capacity of $480\ \mathrm{\mAh\{\g}^{-1}}$ after 1,000 cycles at $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ . Yan and co-workers [28] illustrated the generation of rich structural defects induced by Co doping in $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}/$ graphene nanocomposite, which manifested an enhanced reversible capacity of $636\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 120 cycles at $0.05\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . In another work, Yang’ group [29] addressed the fabrication of heterostructured $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}/\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ nanocubes interlaced with the 3D network of S-doped graphene, delivering a reversible capacity of $758\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and $487\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Similarly, Fu et al. [30] reported the one-step solvothermal synthesis of heterostructured $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}/\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ nanosheet arrays on carbon cloth, which displayed reversible capacities as high as 589 and $343\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at 0.5 and $2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively. More recently, Ci et al. [31] demonstrated the successful conversion of $Z\mathrm{nSn}(\mathrm{OH})_{6}$ into $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanoparticles encapsulated in N-doped hollow carbon boxes with yolk-shell structure, which presented a reversible capacity of $660\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ with a retention of $88.6\%$ after 100 cycles. Although considerable exciting advances have been achieved in the past decade, the electrochemical performance of many $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ -based anode materials is less satisfactory, especially at high rates, and it is still highly essential to develop new $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ -based anodes for SIBs with boosted sodium storage properties.
As a novel class of coordination polymers assembled from metal ions and organic linkers, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been conceived as versatile precursors for fabricating various transition-metal-based compounds owing to their large surface area, high porosities, diverse structures and adjustable compositions [32–37]. To the best of our knowledge, there have been rarely reported researches addressing the combination of MOF-derived metal compounds with $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and the resulting electrochemical performance as anodes of SIBs is worth investigations. Motivated by the above considerations, in this work, we report the solvothermal growth of interconnected $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on MOFderived hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres for the formation of heterostructured $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ as advanced anodes for SIBs. Note that there are several appealing advantages for this delicate design and MOF-engaged strategy. First, the MOF-derived hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres as the nanoreactor can effectively restrain the agglomeration of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ by providing sufficient reaction sites for the growth and firm anchoring of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets. Second, the $\mathsf{N}$ -doped carbon skeleton in-situformed from the carbonization of MOFs can not only improve the electrical conductivity, but also reinforce the structural integrity of the composite by suppressing the shuttling effect of polysulfides with electrolytes and alleviating the volume expansion during the repeated cycling. Third, the hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres with rich porosity and interior void space inherited from the MOF precursor can simultaneously help to buffer the volume variation and stress generated during the cycling process. Fourth, the induced internal electric field by constructing the heterointerface of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ can greatly promote the diffusion of sodium ions in the heterostructure. Last but not the least, the relatively cheap organic ligand of trimesic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ and the green chemistry feature of the formation reaction are highly beneficial for the scalable and facile fabrication of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . By virtue of these advantages, the optimized $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composite exhibits a high reversible capacity of 820 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 250 cycles at $\mathrm{1Ag^{-1}}$ , and retains a high reversible capacity of $673\ \mathrm{mAh\g^{-1}}$ even after 1,300 cycles at ${\mathfrak{s}}\operatorname{A}{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ with a retention of $100\%$ , demonstrating the remarkable rate capability and cycling stability when applied in SIBs. This work may provide a new avenue for the rational design and fabrication of novel hybrid anode materials, which hold great promise for fast and durable sodium-ion storage.
## 2 Results and discussion
The synthetic route for the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composite is schematically illustrated in Figure 1a. Initially, the microstructured Ni-MOF spheres were fabricated via a facile solvothermal reaction with the assistance of polyvinyl pyrrolidone (PVP) as the surfactant (step I) [38], by using $\mathrm{\DeltaH_{3}B T C}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the organic linkers and metal ion source, respectively. In the sulfurization/carbonization process (step II), hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres were prepared in the presence of excess sulfur under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere. During this process, the Ni species were entirely transformed into $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ while the organic linkers were simultaneously carbonized into amorphous carbon skeletons supporting $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ . Note that PVP acted as not only the surfactant for the formation of NiMOF spheres, but also the nitrogen source for doping in the carbon skeleton [39]. Ultimately, interlaced $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets were elaborately grown on the surface of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres by means of a simple solvothermal treatment (step III), resulting in the generation of hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructured composite. It should be mentioned that the composition of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in the hybrid composite can be tailored by adjusting the amount of introduced hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres for the formation of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ under similar reaction conditions. Specifically, by introducing different amounts of hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres (20, 40 and $60~\mathrm{mg}$ ), a series of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composites (labeled as $20{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $40\mathrm{-NiS}_{2}@_{}$ $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $60{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2})$ are obtained, and the mass ratios of hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres to $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets in the composite are determined to be 1:2.3, 1:1.3 and 1:1, respectively. The detailed morphologies and the sodium storage performances of $20{\mathrm{-NiS}}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $60{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are also provided for comparison in Figures S1 and S2. Since the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets exhibit much higher specific capacity and lower electrical conductivity related to hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres, it is reasonable that, from the viewpoint of the electrochemical properties of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructure, there is an optimal content of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets grown on the surface of hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres. In the case of $20{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , it is found that excessive $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets are grown on the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres with severe agglomeration, and a part of these $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets grow separately. Regarding the sample of $60{\cdot}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , only a small amount of large $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets are decorated on the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres. For the sample of $40{\cdot}\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets and hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres with proper contents are well combined in the heterostructure, generating the best electrochemical performance. As a consequence, in this context we select this optimal sample of $40–\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ as an illustrative example for the investigation, which is further denoted as $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in the following context for simplicity.
The morphological transition of the as-prepared samples during the formation process was revealed by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM). As depicted in Figure 1b and Figure S3, the spherical Ni-MOF particles with an average diameter of ca. $1.8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ present a rough surface decorated with numerous cone-shaped structures. After being subjected to a sulfurization/carbonization process, the morphology of uniformly spherical structures is almost inherited by the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ composite without obvious destruction, as ascertained in Figure 1c. From Figure 1d, we can see that a large number of interlaced $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets are nearly vertically aligned on the surface of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ composite, leading to the formation of well-defined core/shell structured $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composite. The magnified SEM image (Figure S4) indicates that each $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheet has a dimension of several hundred nanometers with the thickness less than $20\mathrm{nm}$ , while the hybrid spheres of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ display an enlarged size of ca. $2.4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ due to the coating of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . Figure 1e compares the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the as-prepared $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ samples. As shown in Figure 1e, all the diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ at $27.1^{\circ}$ , $31.4^{\circ}$ , $35.3^{\circ}$ , $38.7^{\circ}$ , $45.0^{\circ}$ , $53.4^{\circ}$ , $58.4^{\circ}$ , $60.9^{\circ}$ are well indexed to the (111), (200), (210), (211), (220), (311), (023) and (321) planes of cubic $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ phase (JCPDS No. 89-3058) [40]. It is noteworthy that no obvious reflections arising from carbon can be detected, suggesting the amorphous feature of the carbon derived from the Ni-MOF within the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ composite [41]. Regarding the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composite, apart from the diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ phase, a series of emerging peaks located at $15.3^{\circ}$ , $28.2^{\circ}$ , $32.0^{\circ}$ , $41.7^{\circ}$ , $50.0^{\circ}$ and $52.5^{\circ}$ are observed, which can be readily assigned to the (001), (100), (101), (102), (110) and (111) planes of hexagonal $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ phase (JCPDS No. 23-0677) [42], similar with those of pristine $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . It is noteworthy that the sample of pristine $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ presents much stronger (001) and (100) peaks at around $15.3^{\circ}$ and $28.2^{\circ}$ related to other peaks, which is probably attributed to the oriented growth of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets with largely exposed (001) and (100) crystal planes. This result is quite different from the standard data of bulk $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ with randomly distributed crystal planes, similar with the previously reported results on $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in literature [43–45]. The XRD pattern of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ clearly verifies the co-existence of both cubic $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and hexagonal $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ phases in the hybrid composite. Based on the above results, we can conclude that the heterostructured $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ is successfully constructed, and the corresponding crystallographic structure is schematically presented in Figure 1f.
More detailed information on the morphology and microstructure of the as-prepared $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ is acquired by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM). Figure 2a and 2b display the typical TEM images of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ at different magnifications, which confirm that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres with hollow interior and rich porosity are actually composed of numerous nanoparticles with the size in the range of $10{-}20~\mathrm{nm}$ . The representative HRTEM image (Figure 2c) reveals two sets of well-resolved lattice fringes with interplanar spacings of $0.33~\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.17\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding well to the (111) and (311) planes of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ , respectively. Besides, the crystalline $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ nanoparticle is closely anchored on an amorphous layer of carbon skeleton as manifested in Figure S5. Figure 2d shows the TEM image of an individual hybrid sphere of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . The lamellar structure of the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets with the thickness of ca. $10\mathrm{nm}$ can be easily distinguished from the curled edge, as shown in Figure 2e. It is noteworthy that this lamellar structure largely displays the (001) plane of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ with an interplanar spacing of $0.59\mathrm{nm}$ (inset of Figure 2e and Figure S6), which is reported as the most favorable facet for the intercalation and de-intercalation of sodium ions in hexagonal $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ during cycling [20,45]. HRTEM observation (Figure 2f) of the heterostructure in $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ shows a series of lattice fringes with interplanar spacings of 0.33, 0.28 and $0.59\mathrm{nm}$ , which are correlated to the (111), (200) planes of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ , and the (001) plane of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , respectively. The elemental mapping analyses (Figure 2g) under high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF–STEM) indicate that the Ni element is detected mainly in the core region, while the Sn and S elements can be identified across the whole nanostructure. Moreover, we also notice that the distribution of C and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ elements is highly overlapped with relative low intensities, demonstrating that the $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ element has been successfully incorporated into the carbon matrix and shielded by the shell materials. Based on the SEM and HRTEM results, we propose a possible growth mechanism of the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on the surface of hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres. At the initial growth stage, the Sn and S atoms are covalently bound and form nuclei on the surface of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ . Due to the much lower surface energy of (001) planes than other planes, the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nuclei show the preferential growth along the (001) plane in parallel with the (200) plane of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ , gradually develop into 2D thin nanosheets with largely exposed (001) basal planes and finally bend outwards to form nearly vertical $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ [46].
The surface composition and valence states of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ composite were probed by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), as illustrated in Figure 3. The survey spectrum indicates that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ composite contains the elements of Ni, S, Sn, C, N and O, as shown in Figure 3a. Note that the occurrence of O element is probably owing to the partial surface oxidation of the metal sulfide and the adsorbed oxygen when the sample is exposed to air [47]. Figure 3b shows the core-level spectrum of $\mathsf{N i}2\mathsf{p}$ consisting of two spin–orbit doublets. Specifically, the fitted peaks at 853.5 and $870.8\mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to the $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}~2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ in sequence, accompanied by two shake-up satellite peaks centered at 859.6 and $876.9\mathrm{eV},$ while the fitted peaks at 855.1 and $872.4\mathrm{eV}$ are indexed to the $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ 2p1/2, respectively, suggesting the partial oxidation of Ni2+ into $\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}$ on the surface [39,48]. In the core-level spectrum of $\mathrm{Sn}\ 3\mathrm{d}$ (Figure 3c), the two distinct peaks at 486.6 and $495.0\mathrm{eV}$ with an energy difference of $8.4\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the $\mathrm{Sn}3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and $\mathrm{Sn}3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ , manifesting the presence of $\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ in the sample [27]. Figure 3d presents the core-level spectrum of $\mathrm{~S~}2\mathrm{p}$ , which can be separated into five component peaks. In detail, the peaks at 161.8 and $162.9\mathrm{eV}$ correspond to the $\mathrm{S}^{2-}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $\mathrm{\bar{S}}^{2-}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ of the $\mathrm{Sn-S}$ bond, while the peaks at 162.6 and $163.8\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the existence of $\mathrm{S}_{2}^{2-}$ in $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ [30,49]. In addition, the broad and weak peak centered at $164.8\mathrm{eV}$ is normally associated with the formation of covalent $\mathrm{C-}\mathrm{S}$ bonds between the carbon matrix and metal sulfide of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . In the case of C 1s spectrum (Figure 3e), the deconvoluted peaks at 284.7, 285.4, 286.8 and $289.0\mathrm{eV}$ are attributed to the $\scriptstyle\operatorname{sp}^{2}\ C=\mathbf{C}/\mathbf{C}-\mathbf{C}$ , C–S, C–N and $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{=}}0$ , respectively [50,51]. In the $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ 1s spectrum (Figure 3f), The three deconvoluted peaks located at 398.7, 400.1 and $401.8\mathrm{eV}$ are corresponding to the pyridinic N, pyrrolic $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ and graphitic N, respectively [31]. As estimated by XPS, the carbon and nitrogen concentrations within the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are calculated to be 41.0 at. $\%$ and $3.4\ \mathrm{at.\%}$ .
Based on the XPS results, we can deduce that a strong interfacial interaction might be formed between the metal sulfide and protective carbon skeleton due to the formation of C–S bonds, leading to the enhanced structural integrity and boosted charge transfer ability in the composite [23]. Besides, the modification of carbon skeleton by $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ doping can not only induce more sufficient active sites for sodiumion insertion, but also further improve the electrical conductivity of the composite [47].
The specific surface area and pore size distribution of the samples are investigated by nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms. As shown in Figure S7a and S7b, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ exhibits a typical type-IV isotherm with a Brunauer–Emmett– Teller (BET) specific surface area of $84.20\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is characteristic of mesoporous structure with pore size concentrated around $16.65\ \mathrm{nm}$ . It is worth mentioning that such a high specific surface area of the hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres with mesoporous structure is greatly beneficial for the nucleation and growth of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ with rich phase boundaries and intimate contact of interfaces. By contrast, the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms and pore size distribution of pristine $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ only provide a BET surface area of $18.68\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ and an average pore size of $3.48\mathrm{nm}$ . In the composite of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres are largely covered by numerous layered $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ shell nanosheets. As a result, it is reasonable that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid spheres are supposed to exhibit moderate specific surface area of $26.48\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and average mesopore size of $3.74\mathrm{nm}$ , which are approaching to the results of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets. It is expected that the large surface area, rich porosity and robust heterostructure of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are favorable for endowing more active sites, promoting the electron/ion transport and alleviating the volume expansion, which are highly desirable for developing fast and durable electrode materials applied in SIBs [52,53].
The sodium storage performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ composite as SIB anodes were first evaluated in a half-cell configuration. Figure 4a shows the initial three cycles of cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves for the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ recorded at a scan rate of $0.1\mathrm{~mV~s~}^{-1}$ in the voltage range of 0.01 to $3.00{\mathrm{V}}.$ For comparison, the pristine $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ were also tested under the same conditions for better clarification, as displayed in Figure S8. During the first sodiation process, a small peak at ${\sim}1.80\mathrm{V}$ is assigned to the intercalation of sodium ions into $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ to generate $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ [21], while a prominent reduction peak observed at $1.28{\mathrm{V}}$ is attributed to the conversion reaction of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ to form $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ , as accompanied by the formation of solid–electrolyte interface (SEI) layers [54]. The broad reduction peaks ranging from 0.90 to $0.32\mathrm{V}$ mainly originate from the further conversion reaction to form $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and metallic $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ and Sn particles, as well as the alloying reaction between Sn and sodium ions [30,31]. As for the desodiation process, a series of oxidation served, which are arising from the dealloying of $_{\mathrm{{Na-Sn}}}$ alloys $(0.28{\mathrm{V}})$ , reverse conversion reaction to form $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(1.23{\mathrm{V}})$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ $(1.70\mathrm{V})$ , and deintercalation reaction to generate $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ $(1.98{\mathrm{V}})$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ (2.14 V), respectively [48,55]. It is noteworthy that the reduction peaks at 1.80 and $1.28{\mathrm{V}},$ , and the oxidation peaks at 1.98 and $1.70\mathrm{V}$ are observed to shift to 1.16, 1.54, 1.90, and $1.66\mathrm{V}$ in the subsequent cycles, respectively, possibly due to the structural rearrangement in the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ [56]. Besides, all the CV curves remain nearly unchanged from the second cycle, reflecting the good electrochemical reversibility of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ composite upon the continuous sodiation/desodiation process. Figure 4b displays the galvanostatic charge– discharge (GCD) curves of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ for the first five cycles at $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Based on the $\mathrm{d}Q/\mathrm{d}V$ profile obtained from the second cycle (Figure S9), it is found that the voltage plateaus in the GCD curves are in good agreement with the CV results. The delivered initial discharge and charge capacities of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are 1,110 and $902\ \mathrm{\mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ corresponding to a high initial Coulombic efficiency (ICE) of $81.2\%$ , which is higher than that of many of the reported $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}.$ - or $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ -based anodes for SIBs (Table S1). Figure 4c depicts the GCD curves of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ at different rates. The distinct and nearly overlapped voltage plateaus undoubtedly indicate the favorable stability and limited polarization of the heterostructure at varied current densities [53]. Figure 4d compares the rate capabilities of the obtained $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ at stepwise current densities ranging from 0.1 to $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . For the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the average reversible capacities of 812, 858, 853, 830, 789, 697 and $638\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ are achieved at current densities of 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5 and $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively, giving rise to a high capacity retention of $78.6\%$ with 100-fold increment of the current density. We propose that the unusual higher capacity at $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ as compared with that at $0.1\mathrm{{Ag}^{\overline{{-}}1}}$ is actually owing to the slow activation process of the electrode materials at the initial stage of testing. More strikingly, when the current density is reset to $0.1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ , a reversible capacity of 824 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ can be recovered, and further increases upon the continuous discharge/charge process. On the contrary, the pristine $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ show much inferior rate capabilities, which quickly decay from 590 and $456\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ to a low value of 167 and $76~\mathrm{mAh~g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively, corresponding to a capacity retention of $28.4\%$ and $16.6\%$ . Compared with the previously reported $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ - or $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ -based anodes for SIBs, the excellent rate performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ can be further illustrated especially at high rates, as displayed in Figure 4e [29–31,40,44,55,57,58].
Except for the rate capability, the cycling stability as another critical parameter is also evaluated to fully demonstrate the advantages of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ (Figure 4f). When tested at $\mathrm{~1~A~g~}^{-1}$ , the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ both suffer from the obvious capacity fading with a relatively poor retention of $63.3\%$ and $35.8\%$ after 250 cycles, respectively, possibly due to the severe volume changes and pulverization of the active materials during sodiation/desodiation. In terms of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\$ $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the reversible capacity experiences a rapid decay in the first several cycles and a period of fluctuation in the next 50 cycles followed by a steady increase afterwards, corresponding to a capacity retention of $119\%$ $\mathrm{820\mAh\g^{-1}},$ ) after 250 cycles. Besides, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ presents an ICE of $81.2\%$ in the first cycle, and the CE quickly increases to $99.3\%$ in the next two cycles. In the following stage, the CE of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ fluctuates between $96.8\%$ and $98.9\%$ before the $25^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle, gradually increases afterwards and finally stabilizes at nearly $100\%$ after 30 cycles. This interesting phenomenon has been rarely reported in metal-sulfide-based anode materials, and we propose that it is approximately associated with the sodiation-induced activation process in such unique hollow core/shell $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ [59]. Upon the initial cycling, the capacity quickly decays due to the structural degradation and the formation of unstable SEI layers. As the continuous cycling proceeds, more open mesoporous structure can be formed and tend to be stable inside the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructure without fracture, which can provide more sufficient active sites for sodium storage, resulting in the gradual increase of the capacity. Similar phenomenon can also be found in hollow metal oxide spheres applied in lithium-ion batteries [60]. Furthermore, the SEM images of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ electrode derived after 250 cycles are provided in Figure S10, indicating that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ still maintains nearly spherical hybrid structure without serious agglomeration or collapse. Figure $4\mathrm{g}$ shows the longterm cycling performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ tested at a high current density of ${\mathfrak{s}}\operatorname{A}{\mathfrak{g}}^{-1}$ after 30 cycles of activation at $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Notably, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ yields an extraordinarily high cycling stability even after 1,300 cycles, which still retains a reversible capacity of $673\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ with nearly $100\%$ retention and a high CE of $99.8\%$ .
In addition, the electrochemical properties of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@ $ $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ as SIB anodes were also investigated in a conventional carbonate electrolyte $\mathrm{~\stackrel{~1~}{~m o l~L}^{-1}~}$ sodium hexafluorophosphate $\left(\mathrm{NaPF}_{6}\right)$ in ethylene carbonate (EC):diethyl carbonate $\scriptstyle\mathrm{(DEC)=1:1}\ \mathrm{vol.\}\%,$ , as shown in Figure S11. Notably, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ exhibits a rather low ICE of $41.8\%$ , and the capacity dramatically decreases from 684 to $138\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 50 cycles at a current of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . It is suggested that the much inferior sodium storage performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@$ $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in carbonate-based electrolytes as compared with that in ether-based electrolytes might be attributed to the elevated desolvation barrier of sodium ions at the electrode/electrolyte interface, and the formation of a thicker SEI layer on the surface of the electrode in the carbonate-based electrolytes, which hinders the diffusion of sodium ions into the anode during cycling [61,62].
To better interpret the outstanding rate capability and cycling performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the electrochemical reaction kinetics was explored by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). Figure 5a shows the Nyquist plots of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ tested in fresh cells at the open-circuit voltage state. All these plots are normally featured by a depressed semicircle and an inclined straight line in the high- and low-frequency regions, respectively, which are closely related to the charge-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ on the electrode/electrolyte or different electrode interfaces, and Warburg impedance of sodium ion diffusion $(Z_{\mathrm{w}})$ [63]. Based on the fitting data of the equivalent circuit model (inset of Figure 5a), it is revealed that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ exhibits a much lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $10.8\Omega$ than $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ $(21.8\Omega)$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(23.9\ \Omega)$ , confirming the much enhanced charge transfer ability by the integration of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ within the hybrid electrode. Besides, the sodium-ion diffusion coefficients $({D_{\mathrm{Na}}}^{+})$ of the electrodes can be assessed from the Warburg region according to the following equation:
$$
{D_{\mathrm{Na}}}^{+}=0.5R^{2}T^{2}/S^{2}n^{4}F^{4}C^{2}\sigma^{2}
$$
where $R$ and $T$ represent the gas constant and absolute temperature, $S$ is the electrode area, $n$ is the number of reaction electrons, $C$ is the concentration of sodium ions in the electrolyte, $F$ is the Faraday constant, while the Warburg factor $(\sigma)$ can be calculated from the fitting line slope by plotting $Z^{\prime}$ versus $\omega^{-1/2}$ [64]. As shown in Figure 5b, the values of $\sigma$ for $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are obtained to be 24.27, 55.32 and 42.58, respectively. As a result, the ${D_{\mathrm{Na}}}^{+}$ value of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(6.11{\times}\mathrm{\bar{10}}^{-14}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ is distinctly larger than that of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ $(3.36{\times}10^{-14}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(6.99{\times}10^{-15}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ , demonstrating the better sodium ion diffusion kinetics in the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ . Figure 5c compares the EIS spectra of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in fresh cell at open circuit voltage (OCV) and after various cycles at fully charged states as tested at a current density of $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . It is revealed that the $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ quickly decreases from the initial $10.8\Omega$ to a minimal value of $6.0\Omega$ after the ${10}^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle, and then presents a trend of slow increase to $19.2\ \Omega$ after the $250^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle. Since the EIS spectra acquired at fully discharged states might provide more information of the electrodes including the formation and change of the SEI layer, we will investigate this issue in our future work. Besides, based on the fitting slopes by plotting $Z^{\prime}$ versus −1/2 (Figure 5d and Table S2), the variation of ${D_{\mathrm{Na}}}^{+}$ value for the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ upon cycling can also be obtained, which increases from the initial $6.11{\times}10^{-14}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ to $4.18{\times}10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ after the ${10}^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle, and then gradually decreases and still retains $1.23{\times}10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ after the $250^{\mathrm{th}}$ cycle (Figure 5e). We propose that the transition of $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ and ${D_{\mathrm{Na}}}^{+}$ values upon cycling in the initial stage is probably associated with the slow activation and structural rearrangement of the hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ composite. During the whole cycling process, the enhanced charge transfer ability and ion diffusion kinetics show no significant deterioration, which can facilitate the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode to deliver good electrochemical performance [65].
Additionally, the detailed reaction kinetics of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\$ $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ was also investigated by CV measurement. Figure 5f displays the CV profiles of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ at different scan rates from 0.2 to $1\mathrm{mV~s}^{-1}$ , which exhibit very similar contours at all scan rates. As we know, the pseudocapacitive and diffusion-controlled behaviors can be qualitatively evaluated by the slope $b$ according to the relationship between the peak current and scan rate as follows [14]:
