To gain further insights into the sodium storage reaction mechanism in $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , ex situ XRD characterization was performed in the initial sodiation/desodiation process at a current density of $0.05\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ between 0.01 and $3.0\mathrm{V}.$ The collected ex situ XRD data are displayed in 2D contour and line patterns in Figure 6a and 6b, respectively. In the fresh electrode at OCV, the main peaks of both $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ are clearly identified. As the voltage decreases from OCV to $1.1\mathrm{V},$ the peaks related to $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ almost disappear at 1.6 and $1.1\mathrm{V}$ in sequence, respectively, corresponding to the intercalation of sodium ions into $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ with the formation of $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ [44]. With the voltage decreasing to $0.8\mathrm{V},$ the characteristic (220) peak of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ (JCPDS No. 23-0441) appears at $38.5^{\circ}$ and a new broad peak emerges at about $44.5^{\circ}$ attributed to the (111) plane of metallic Ni (JCPDS No. 45-1027), suggesting the conversion reaction of $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and metallic Ni [39]. When the battery is further discharged to 0.01 V, $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ is converted into $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and Sn, accompanied by the alloying reaction between Sn and sodium ions to form Na–Sn alloys. It is found that the peak of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ becomes much stronger in intensity due to the increased content from the conversion reaction of $\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , while the signals of Sn and $\mathrm{\DeltaNa-Sn}$ alloys are hardly detectable from the XRD patterns, which are probably attributed to the low crystallinity and small particle size of the generated Sn and $_{\mathrm{\DeltaNa-Sn}}$ alloy [21]. When recharged back to $0.8\mathrm{V}_{:}$ , the peak of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ gradually decreases while three obvious peaks are readily observed at about $30.6^{\circ}$ , $32.0^{\circ}$ and $45.0^{\circ}$ , which are well indexed to the (200), (101) and (211) planes of metallic Sn phase (JCPDS No. 04- 0673) [68], signifying that the dealloying reaction of $_{\mathrm{Na-Sn}}$ alloys takes place to form metallic Sn particles. When the voltage is further increased to 1.8 and $3.0\mathrm{V}_{:}$ the peaks of metallic $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ and Sn have completely disappeared, indicating the reverse conversion reaction between $\mathrm{\DeltaNi/Sn}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ to regenerate $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ . Nevertheless, no distinct peaks for $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ are observed after the desodiation process, probably due to the poor crystallinity of the formed $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ randomly distributed in the matrix [69,70]. Additionally, the presence of trace amount of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ as identified from the XRD pattern upon charging to $3.0\mathrm{V}$ might demonstrate the partially reversible conversion reaction of electrode materials [27]. Based on the analyses of CV and ex situ XRD results, the charge storage mechanism of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode can be inferred as the following formula:
$$
\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{Ni}\mathrm{S}_{2}+(4-x)\mathrm{Na}^{+}+(4-x)\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Ni}+2\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Na}_{x}\mathrm{SnS}_{2}+(4-x)\mathrm{Na}^{+}+(4-x)\mathrm{e}^{-}\leftrightarrow\mathrm{Sn}+2\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}
$$
To further explore the effect of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterointerface on the reaction kinetics, DFT calculations were applied to elucidate the interfacial charge behaviors. Based on the SEM and HRTEM results, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructure model was constructed based on the (200) surface of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and (001) surface of $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ with the optimized geometry models of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ included for comparison as shown in Figure S12. According to the calculated density of states (DOS) analysis (Figure 7a), it is revealed that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ as a $p$ -type semiconductor exhibits a narrow bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $0.32\mathrm{eV}_{:}$ while the $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ is a $n$ -type semiconductor with a relatively wide $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of $1.56\mathrm{eV}$ . In the heterostructured $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the DOS pattern presents a continuous distribution around the Fermi level with no obvious $E_{\mathrm{g}},$ reflecting the much enhanced electrical conductivity of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ [71]. In addition, the intimate contact between different types of semiconductors usually results in unbalanced charge distribution across the interface [29,66]. From the charge density difference of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ as shown in Figure $76$ , one can see that the electrons tend to migrate from $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ and accumulate on the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ side, indicating the formation of an internal electric field at the interface. It is proposed that this internal electric field can facilitate the migration of sodium ions through Coulomb forces and remarkably accelerate the charge transfer kinetics, as schematically described in Figure 7d [72]. The sodium ion adsorption energies $(E_{\mathrm{ads}})$ of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ are also calculated and compared in Figure $\mathrm{7c}$ . The sodium ion adsorption energy of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(-2.08\mathrm{eV})$ is much lower than that of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ $(-0.97\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ $(-0.51\ \mathrm{eV})$ , suggesting that the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructure is more favorable for the adsorption of sodium ions in the system and beneficial for the improvement of sodium storage capacity of the anode [73]. Therefore, by constructing the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterointerface, the enhanced electrical conductivity, favorable adsorption of sodium ions and accelerated charge transfer kinetics could mutually boost the electrochemical properties with good rate capability in virtue of the well-designed $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ heterostructure.
Inspired by the remarkable electrochemical properties of the as-prepared $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode in half-cell, full batteries based on $\mathrm{NiS_{2}@S n S_{2}||N a_{3}V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}}$ (NVP) were assembled to further evaluate thier practical application toward SIBs. Figure 8a displays a schematic diagram of the composition and structure for the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}||\mathrm{NVP}$ device by coupling the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode with the homemade NASICON-type NVP cathode. In order to realize the maximum utilization and reasonably evaluate the electrochemical performance of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ , the full battery was assembled with a capacity ratio of 1:1.2 between the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode and NVP cathode [55,65]. Figure 8b shows the charge–discharge profiles of the NVP and $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ in the half-cell with distinct plateaus. Based on their voltage difference, a suitable voltage window of $0.5{-}3.5\mathrm{V}$ was applied for the electrochemical measurement of the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}||\mathrm{I}$ NVP-based full battery [74]. Before the assembly of the full battery, the anode of $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ was presodiated between 0.01 and $3.00\mathrm{V}$ at $0.2\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ for three cycles to compensate the deficiency of active sodium ions in the anode and the fast consumption in electrolytes during the formation of SEI layers [75]. Figure 8c illustrates the charge–discharge curves of the as-prepared full battery for the first five cycles tested at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . The first discharge and charge capacities of 798 and $753\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ are achieved based on the mass of the active anode materials, accompanied by an extraordinarily high ICE of $94.3\%$ . Besides, the good reversibility of the full battery can be validated by the almost overlapped charge– discharge profiles with a high CE $(>97.5\%)$ from the third cycle. Figure 8d compares the cycling performances of the full battery with and without the presodiation treatment. From Figure 8d, it is revealed that the specific capacity, cycling stability and CE of the full battery are all promoted to some extent after presodiation, which retains a considerably high reversible capacity of $628\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}}$ at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles with a capacity retention of $80.4\%$ . By contrast, the full battery without presodiation shows rapid capacity decay with a low value of $282\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 150 cycles with retention of $51.4\%$ . Moreover, the rate performance of the full battery can also be further improved by presodiation (Figure 8e), which delivers an average reversible capacity of 740, 638 593, 554, 507, and $322\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 0.1, 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2 and $5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , respectively. The electrochemical performance of the full battery based on the total mass of active materials on the anode and cathode are provided in Figure S13. As an important parameter for considering the commercialization of anodes for practical application, the volumetric capacities of the full battery were calculated based on the average volume of the anode material [76]. As shown in Figure S14, the $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ anode in the full battery with presodiation exhibits a volumetric capacity of $785\mathrm{\mAh\cm}^{-3}$ after 150 cycles at $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and still retains a rate capability of $403\mathrm{mAh}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ at $5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Figure 8f depicts the Ragone plots of the $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}\|\mathrm{NVP}.}\end{array}$ -based full battery, unveiling the relationship between the power and energy densities, in comparison with those of some recently reported SIBs in literature [68,77–81]. Herein, the gravimetric energy and power densities of the full battery are calculated based on the total mass of active materials on the anode and cathode. It is found that the full battery exhibits a high energy density of $274\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $52.1~\mathrm{W~kg}^{-1}$ , and still delivers an energy density of $119\mathrm{Wh}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ even at a high power density of $2.6\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , showing the great potential of our $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}||$ NVP-based full battery in real applications.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, heterostructured $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid spheres were elaborately designed and successfully fabricated by anchoring $\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ nanosheets on MOF-derived hollow $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ spheres by the combined solvothermal, sulfurization and carbonization processes. Benefitting from the robust structure, enhanced electrical conductivity and accelerated charge transfer kinetics as evidenced by experimental and theoretical results, the optimized $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}@\mathrm{SnS}_{2}$ hybrid composite delivers high reversible capacity $(820\mathrm{~mAh~g^{-1}~}$ after 250 cycles at ${\mathfrak{l}}\operatorname{Ag}^{-1}\gamma$ , unprecedented rate capability $(638\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}\big>$ ) and excellent cycling stability ( $100\%$ retention at $5.0\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ over 1,300 cycles) as anodes in SIBs. We anticipate that the synthetic strategy involved in this work might shed more light on the rational design and exploitation of novel binary-metal sulfides as advanced anodes for high rate and durable SIB devices.
